    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p39-q28-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that if $h$ is the perpendicular height of the triangle, then $h=a+b+c$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h=a+b+c$.\n\nJoin $P$ to the vertices and equate the sum of the three triangle areas to $\\tfrac12sh$.",
          "worked": "Let $s$ be the side length and $T$ the area of $\\triangle ABC$. Join $P$ to $A$, $B$ and $C$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}T&=\\frac12sa+\\frac12sb+\\frac12sc\\\\T&=\\frac12s(a+b+c).\\end{align*}$$\n\nUsing the perpendicular height of the whole triangle,\n\n$$T=\\frac12sh.$$\n\nEquating the areas and dividing by $\\tfrac12s$, which is positive,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac12sh&=\\frac12s(a+b+c)\\\\h&=a+b+c.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q28-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Now, let $\\angle APB=\\alpha$, $\\angle BPC=\\beta$, $\\angle CPA=\\gamma$. Also, let $AP=x$, $BP=y$ and $CP=z$. Show that\n\n$$xy\\sin\\alpha+yz\\sin\\beta+zx\\sin\\gamma=\\frac12s^2\\sqrt3,$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the equilateral triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$xy\\sin\\alpha+yz\\sin\\beta+zx\\sin\\gamma=\\tfrac12s^2\\sqrt3$.\n\nSum the three included-angle area expressions and equate their total to $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}s^2$.",
          "worked": "The altitude of an equilateral triangle bisects its base. By Pythagoras’ theorem,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h^2&=s^2-\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2\\\\h&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}s.\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence the whole triangle has area\n\n$$\\begin{align*}T&=\\frac12s\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}s\\right)\\\\T&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}s^2.\\end{align*}$$\n\nJoining $P$ to the vertices gives three triangles. Applying the area rule to each gives\n\n$$T=\\frac12\\left(xy\\sin\\alpha+yz\\sin\\beta+zx\\sin\\gamma\\right).$$\n\nEquating the two expressions for $T$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac12\\left(xy\\sin\\alpha+yz\\sin\\beta+zx\\sin\\gamma\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}s^2\\\\xy\\sin\\alpha+yz\\sin\\beta+zx\\sin\\gamma&=\\frac12s^2\\sqrt3.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      }
    ]
